msgid "Acknowledgements"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus has long supported both JVM and native image modes, each optimized "
"for different trade-offs.\n"
"Native image delivers unmatched startup and footprint.\n"
"JVM mode offers peak throughput, dynamic capabilities, and a mature tooling "
"ecosystem."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus continues to improve and work well with native-image,\n"
"and lately Project Leyden enabled us to strengthen the JVM side of that "
"equation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We shared our excitement about Project Leyden and how it gave us a new "
"perspective on Java application startup performance in a previous https://"
"quarkus.io/blog/leyden-1/[blog post]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In this post, we want to share our journey integrating Project Leyden into "
"Quarkus, and how we made this integration both efficient and easy to use for "
"our users."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"First, we would like to thank the OpenJDK team at IBM for their work on "
"Project Leyden and for the many discussions we had with them,\n"
"which were instrumental in helping us understand Leyden and determine how "
"best to integrate it into Quarkus,\n"
"namely (in alphabetical order), Maria Arias de Reyna Dominguez, Andrew Dinn, "
"and Ashutosh Mehra."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"More broadly, we would like to thank everyone contributing to Project Leyden."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Project Leyden is still evolving, and we look forward to seeing what they "
"are preparing for Java 27 and beyond."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Finally, special thanks to Sanne Grinovero for introducing us to the Leyden "
"team at just the right time, and for his insights and valuable feedback on "
"our work."
msgstr ""

msgid "What is Project Leyden?"
msgstr ""

msgid "GraalVM and the popularization of AOT in Java"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the Java world, Ahead-of-Time (AOT) compilation rose to prominence with "
"the introduction of GraalVM native image, which compiles Java applications "
"into native executables."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It made a huge impact on the Java ecosystem and was a game changer for Java "
"application startup performance:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Startup time and memory footprint are significantly reduced."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"There is no warmup phase, as the code is already compiled to native code. "
"Performance is effectively at its peak from the start."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The resulting executable is small, leading to much smaller container images."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Resident Set Size (RSS) memory usage is much smaller compared to a "
"regular JVM application."
msgstr ""

msgid "But this comes with some drawbacks:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Not all Java features and libraries are supported out of the box as they are "
"on OpenJDK."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It relies on a closed-world assumption and removes most runtime dynamism (at "
"least in its current form - work on the GraalVM side could change this in "
"the future). Note: This was not and is not an issue for Quarkus as it makes "
"the same assumption; it can be problematic for third-party libraries."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It introduces specific constraints, the most common being the need to "
"provide explicit reflection configuration. These requirements add effort "
"during development and testing, although they are minimized for Quarkus "
"supported extensions."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Native image builds are significantly slower. This typically does not affect "
"the inner development loop (except when debugging native-specific issues), "
"but it can have a noticeable impact on CI resources."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Debugging native executables is hard. It has improved, but it is still a lot "
"harder than debugging in the JVM as it requires the use of GDB and the "
"presence of debug symbols in the build."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"And since everything is AOT-compiled, there is no JIT compiler to optimize "
"code at runtime, which can lead to suboptimal peak performance in some cases."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All in all, native image is an excellent option when startup time and "
"footprint are the primary goals."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"However, not all applications optimize for those dimensions.\n"
"Some prioritize peak throughput, development velocity, debuggability, and "
"compatibility with existing tooling.\n"
"This is where Leyden becomes interesting, aiming to improve startup and "
"footprint while staying closer to the traditional JVM model."
msgstr ""

msgid "Project Leyden&#8217;s approach to AOT"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Project Leyden addresses the same fundamental problem as GraalVM native "
"image, but takes a different approach:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It is still \"pure\" Java, it is part of OpenJDK: your application runs on "
"the JVM, with full access to Java features and libraries, without special "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"During a training phase, it records application behavior and gathers "
"information such as loaded and linked classes, and in more recent versions "
"also starts recording some JIT output - increasing scope and optimization "
"potentials in each JVM version."
msgstr ""

msgid "This information is stored in an AOT cache file."
msgstr ""

msgid "At startup, you configure the JVM with this AOT cache file."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Because the application still runs on the JVM, you continue to benefit from "
"the JIT compiler, your preferred garbage collector, and all other JVM "
"optimizations, preserving the high throughput typically associated with the "
"JVM."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The AOT cache is exactly that: a cache. If a class is not present in the "
"cache, it is loaded and linked as usual."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Project Leyden reduces startup time by optimizing class loading and linking.\n"
"It reduces warmup time by providing the JVM with profiling information, and "
"will reduce it even further once compiled code itself is stored in the cache."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "How Leyden works"
msgstr ""

msgid "That said, Project Leyden is not magic either. In its current form:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Startup and warmup time improvements are significant, but not as substantial "
"as with GraalVM/Mandrel native image."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You must train your application and generate the AOT cache. In practice, "
"this is not difficult, and in Quarkus, we have made it as seamless as "
"possible."
msgstr ""

msgid "You still need to ship a JVM with your application."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You also need to ship the AOT cache, which means container images will be "
"significantly larger than with native executables."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You don't get any memory footprint benefit yet: in most cases, it's "
"comparable to a regular JVM."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Our take is that Project Leyden offers a compelling balance between "
"performance and compatibility, at a very reasonable cost,\n"
"and could become part of the default deployment workflow for many Java "
"applications running on the JVM."
msgstr ""

msgid "Project Leyden status"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In OpenJDK 25, the following Project Leyden-related JEPs are available:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://openjdk.org/jeps/483[JEP 483 - Ahead-of-Time Class Loading & "
"Linking]"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://openjdk.org/jeps/514[JEP 514 - Ahead-of-Time Command-Line "
"Ergonomics]"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://openjdk.org/jeps/515[JEP 515 - Ahead-of-Time Method Profiling]"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Together, these JEPs make Project Leyden fully usable starting with OpenJDK "
"25."
msgstr ""

msgid "Additional JEPs are planned for OpenJDK 26 and beyond."
msgstr ""

msgid "Project Leyden in Quarkus"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus is a highly specialized framework, with extensive build-time "
"processing and optimizations.\n"
"Project Leyden is a specialized technology as well, with its own constraints "
"and requirements."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Our goal was to integrate Project Leyden into Quarkus in a way that "
"preserves what Quarkus does best, while also maximizing the benefits of "
"Leyden."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus 3.32 includes the first version of our end-to-end Leyden integration."
" Let's take a closer look at how it works."
msgstr ""

msgid "AOT JAR packaging"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You may be used to seeing your Quarkus application packaged in a `target/"
"quarkus-app/` directory, but that wasn't always the case.\n"
"In the early days of Quarkus, we built a traditional runner JAR in `target/"
"`, with dependencies placed in a `lib/` directory, much like most other Java "
"frameworks."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The current default packaging in Quarkus is called `fast-jar`.\n"
"One of its primary goals is to optimize class loading."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We achieved this by introducing our own class loader, which, for example, "
"keeps a `package name` -> `jar file` mapping in memory.\n"
"It also includes additional optimizations, such as maintaining a full "
"directory index for specific locations like `META-INF/services`.\n"
"These are just a few examples of what this packaging does, but they should "
"give you some context."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This packaging performs very well in the traditional JVM world, but it "
"doesn't play nicely with Project Leyden."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Project Leyden takes a conservative approach: it only caches classes loaded "
"by the standard JDK class loaders.\n"
"In other words, no custom class loaders are allowed if you want to fully "
"benefit from AOT caching."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We hope that, at some point, this limitation will be lifted by the Leyden "
"team.\n"
"If that happens, we will return to the `fast-jar` packaging, or an evolution "
"of it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To be precise: you cannot use custom class loaders to load **classes** if "
"you want to fully benefit from AOT caching.\n"
"However, you can still use a custom class loader for loading **resources** "
"only."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is why we developed a dedicated packaging for Project Leyden: `aot-jar`."
"\n"
"It is automatically selected when AOT is enabled, making the transition "
"completely transparent.\n"
"It uses the same file layout as the `fast-jar` packaging."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In addition to delegating all **class** loading to the JDK class loader, "
"this packaging has a few specific characteristics:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It collects service descriptors from `META-INF/services`, aggregates them, "
"and keeps them in memory."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It applies the same approach to Quarkus configuration files (e.g., "
"`application.properties`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It maintains a full index of specific directories (e.g., the root directory, "
"`META-INF`, and `META-INF/services`)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It introduces a pre-initialization phase, during which selected elements are "
"preloaded in parallel (e.g., the time zone database)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As this packaging format is new, we hope to gather feedback from real-world "
"usage."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Let's be clear: `aot-jar` performs worse than `fast-jar` when Project Leyden "
"is not used."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"It does not include all the optimizations of `fast-jar` and is intended to "
"be used only in conjunction with Project Leyden.\n"
"So it should not be used outside of this specific use case."
msgstr ""

msgid "Training and AOT cache generation"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The AOT cache is only as good as the training data used to generate it.\n"
"During the training phase, the JVM records which classes are loaded and "
"linked, and collects method profiling data.\n"
"The more representative the training workload, the more effective the "
"resulting cache."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We wanted training to be as seamless as possible for Quarkus users.\n"
"The natural fit was to leverage the existing integration test infrastructure:"
"\n"
"if you already have `@QuarkusIntegrationTest` tests, they can serve as the "
"training workload."
msgstr ""

msgid "When AOT training is enabled, the flow is as follows:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus builds the application with the `aot-jar` packaging."
msgstr ""

msgid "The application starts with AOT training enabled."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Integration tests run against the application, exercising its endpoints and "
"features."
msgstr ""

msgid "The JVM captures profiling data during the entire test execution."
msgstr ""

msgid "An optimized `app.aot` cache file is generated from the recorded data."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is both practical and effective: you already write integration tests to "
"validate your application,\n"
"and they also happen to produce somewhat realistic training data for the AOT "
"cache."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Integration tests that use `@QuarkusIntegrationTest` make it very easy to "
"run the target application as they use Dev Services."
msgstr ""

msgid "Integration with the build system"
msgstr ""

msgid "We wanted enabling Leyden in Quarkus to be a one-flag operation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Setting `quarkus.package.jar.aot.enabled=true` is all it takes.\n"
"Quarkus automatically switches to the `aot-jar` packaging and sets up the "
"entire AOT pipeline."
msgstr ""

msgid "With Maven, the full flow is a single command:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This builds the application, runs integration tests with AOT training, and "
"generates the `app.aot` cache file in `target/quarkus-app/`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you are using a project generated by our tooling, integration tests are "
"disabled by default.\n"
"Adding `-DskipITs=false` makes sure they will be run."
msgstr ""

msgid "Running the application with the AOT cache is just as straightforward:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We also integrated AOT with the container image extensions (Jib, Docker, and "
"Podman).\n"
"When building a container image with AOT enabled, Quarkus:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Builds the application with `aot-jar` packaging."
msgstr ""

msgid "Creates a base container image."
msgstr ""

msgid "Runs integration tests against the container to train the AOT cache."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Produces a final container image (with an `-aot` suffix in its version) that "
"includes the AOT cache and is pre-configured to use it at startup."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This end-to-end integration means going from source code to an AOT-optimized "
"container image in a single command."
msgstr ""

msgid "Some numbers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"We've described the benefits of Project Leyden in theory, but how does it "
"perform in practice?"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To find out, we collected some numbers for two different Quarkus "
"applications:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A simple REST application, the one you get when you run `quarkus create app`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A very large REST CRUD application: 1,000 entities, 1,000 repositories, "
"1,000 services, 1,000 REST endpoints... `9,000` .java files in total. This "
"is an extreme case, don't try this at home!"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The container image sizes were measured using our default images, which are "
"based on Red Hat's UBI 9 Minimal and use JDK 25."
msgstr ""

msgid "Raw numbers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"These numbers were obtained using a full recording of all classes loaded "
"during startup.\n"
"They were measured on our laptops rather than in an isolated lab environment."
"\n"
"We plan to run a more comprehensive set of benchmarks in our lab soon."
msgstr ""

msgid "Your results may vary for several reasons:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Not all classes required at startup were recorded."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your application performs actual work during startup, Leyden only optimizes "
"class loading, not application logic."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In any case, if you see unexpected results, we're very interested in your "
"feedback.\n"
"Please reach out to us, and we'll guide you on how to gather useful "
"profiling information."
msgstr ""

